{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2203 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "fb9fcf4b32724f1e3f6fda532e96ff38f5c4d0265ef7d4355508d116c97b821c" :edb/db-before-t 2202 :edb/db-after-t 2203 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535515 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:38:26.725Z" 13194139535515 true] [17592186166795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353117 13194139535515 true] [17592186166795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353138 13194139535515 true] [17592186166795 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353145 13194139535515 true] [17592186166796 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353124 13194139535515 true] [17592186166796 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353131 13194139535515 true] [17592186353117 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353118 13194139535515 true] [17592186353117 1089 "q-104573" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353117 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535515 true] [17592186353117 1100 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=(2x-y)\\,i-x\\,j$\n- $G=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j$\n- $H={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j$" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353117 1113 #uuid "eda2d0ee-3996-546b-8940-0cf2e5c82214" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353117 1145 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=2x-y$ and $Q=-x.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={e}^{x-y}$ and $Q=-{e}^{x-y}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{e}^{x-y},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{e}^{x-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353118 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353119 13194139535515 true] [17592186353118 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353120 13194139535515 true] [17592186353118 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353121 13194139535515 true] [17592186353118 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353122 13194139535515 true] [17592186353118 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353123 13194139535515 true] [17592186353118 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535515 true] [17592186353118 1041 "selection" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353118 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353119 13194139535515 true] [17592186353118 1110 #uuid "73570a8e-83b4-5052-a1f9-458da445301f" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353119 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353119 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535515 true] [17592186353119 1111 #uuid "5617ea2d-f782-5963-9718-3b54d9542ba2" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353120 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353120 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535515 true] [17592186353120 1111 #uuid "8e7f1fe7-8b7e-5835-981e-5809d212cb16" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353121 1034 "I only" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353121 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535515 true] [17592186353121 1111 #uuid "9884021c-57bd-53fb-9d7d-34d167e2b748" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353122 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353122 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535515 true] [17592186353122 1111 #uuid "efc4017e-2118-5bf9-b18d-a17d34f09a32" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353123 1034 "II only" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353123 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535515 true] [17592186353123 1111 #uuid "f530a0dc-6b47-5dc0-9e6d-a9bf73b04bc4" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353124 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353125 13194139535515 true] [17592186353124 1089 "q-123993" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353124 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535515 true] [17592186353124 1100 "Consider the vector field $F=({y}^{3}-ay)\\,i+(3x{y}^{2}-2x)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353124 1113 #uuid "b78ea9c7-6cb3-5be5-a80b-26c5a7ca735f" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353124 1145 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP={y}^{3}-ay,\\,Q=3x{y}^{2}-2x.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-a,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{y}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}^{2}-a & =3{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ -a & =-2 \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=2.$" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353126 13194139535515 true] [17592186353125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353127 13194139535515 true] [17592186353125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353128 13194139535515 true] [17592186353125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353129 13194139535515 true] [17592186353125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353130 13194139535515 true] [17592186353125 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535515 true] [17592186353125 1041 "selection" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353125 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353128 13194139535515 true] [17592186353125 1110 #uuid "b4a2569e-275f-56cc-8cd2-cc1cba396bfb" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353126 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353126 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535515 true] [17592186353126 1111 #uuid "5d145467-e24d-5ee0-8159-037cdc84a93c" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353127 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353127 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535515 true] [17592186353127 1111 #uuid "ff2c4747-3e33-5106-8b2e-4a767b9bc29e" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353128 1034 "2" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535515 true] [17592186353128 1111 #uuid "fc5d8ebd-ba5d-542f-97d7-83331e162bc5" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353129 1034 "1" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353129 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535515 true] [17592186353129 1111 #uuid "17fb6843-767b-548e-920b-84c595a1a777" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353130 1034 "-2" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353130 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535515 true] [17592186353130 1111 #uuid "1b545968-ea7c-5961-81d2-e92d370aa7ba" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353131 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353132 13194139535515 true] [17592186353131 1089 "q-124000" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353131 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535515 true] [17592186353131 1100 "Consider the vector field $F=(ax{y}^{2}+1)\\,i+(4{x}^{2}y+1)\\,j$ on ${R}^{2}$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $F$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353131 1113 #uuid "d2220289-1111-5b52-be6c-71da4a389a5e" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353131 1145 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $F,$ the domain $D={R}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=ax{y}^{2}+1,\\,Q=4{x}^{2}y+1.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2axy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8xy.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${R}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2axy & =8xy \\\\ 2axy-8xy & =0 \\\\ 2(a-4)xy & =0 \\\\ a-4 & =0 \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the last equality must be satisfied everywhere on $D,$ we conclude that $a-4=0,$ that is, $a=4.$" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353133 13194139535515 true] [17592186353132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353134 13194139535515 true] [17592186353132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353135 13194139535515 true] [17592186353132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353136 13194139535515 true] [17592186353132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353137 13194139535515 true] [17592186353132 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535515 true] [17592186353132 1041 "selection" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353132 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353137 13194139535515 true] [17592186353132 1110 #uuid "36b7053d-a5e0-5f1a-ba9d-c1a705a70d78" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353133 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353133 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535515 true] [17592186353133 1111 #uuid "5f30b466-a2b0-5a80-8ed3-254d4abf18fd" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353134 1034 "8" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353134 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535515 true] [17592186353134 1111 #uuid "92463822-45c4-5004-8992-c757d5713cce" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353135 1034 "2" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535515 true] [17592186353135 1111 #uuid "c8656e1e-770d-5967-aae2-8d1fcdd00102" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353136 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535515 true] [17592186353136 1111 #uuid "26a2905d-f9e1-5dff-b79c-afe731aabf1e" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353137 1034 "4" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535515 true] [17592186353137 1111 #uuid "99ff0fb5-1765-544c-b886-cd3e08ef758e" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353138 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353139 13194139535515 true] [17592186353138 1089 "q-81743" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353138 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535515 true] [17592186353138 1100 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$\n- $G=(2x+1)\\,i+(5{y}^{2}+2y+1)\\,j$\n- $H={y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}\\,j$" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353138 1113 #uuid "cdb58755-dce7-53fb-b02d-87487036a40e" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353138 1145 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=2x+1$ and $Q=5{y}^{2}+2y+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P={y}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353140 13194139535515 true] [17592186353139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353141 13194139535515 true] [17592186353139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353142 13194139535515 true] [17592186353139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353143 13194139535515 true] [17592186353139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353144 13194139535515 true] [17592186353139 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535515 true] [17592186353139 1041 "selection" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353139 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353144 13194139535515 true] [17592186353139 1110 #uuid "22fed861-88c3-587f-92ec-ea574fffa4af" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353140 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353140 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535515 true] [17592186353140 1111 #uuid "1c4b8086-5c3a-5371-925d-d54d5dfab4d0" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353141 1034 "I only" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353141 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535515 true] [17592186353141 1111 #uuid "b93bb90c-906f-58ea-bfa8-552b5da9d900" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353142 1034 "III only" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353142 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535515 true] [17592186353142 1111 #uuid "b92df2dd-347b-5181-8c8b-209445fa6d06" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353143 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353143 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535515 true] [17592186353143 1111 #uuid "9728504d-bd7a-5aa6-9f45-1ee03c58f18a" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353144 1034 "II only" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353144 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535515 true] [17592186353144 1111 #uuid "ac787dbe-72fc-5f3a-953b-7d5e09bc13bc" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353145 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353146 13194139535515 true] [17592186353145 1089 "q-81746" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353145 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535515 true] [17592186353145 1100 "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $F=xy\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j$\n- $G=(4x+{y}^{2})\\,i-4x{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $H=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,j$" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353145 1113 #uuid "6e3188a0-14a9-5251-b7b1-7ab83fae9c44" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353145 1145 "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.  \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=xy$ and $Q=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=4x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$ and $Q=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535515 true] [17592186353146 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353147 13194139535515 true] [17592186353146 